WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.130
Open WebUI can be deployed on a local workstation, a homelab server,

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:05.600
or a shared internal system.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:09.600
A workstation deployment is useful for personal experimentation and quick learning.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.100
A homelab server can provide a more stable always-on workspace for private AI services.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.475
A shared deployment can support multiple users,

00:00:16.475 --> 00:00:20.590
but it requires more attention to accounts, permissions, storage, backups, updates,

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:21.880
and exposure control.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.260
Container-based deployment is common at a high level because it helps package the

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.000
application and its dependencies.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.100
Containers do not remove the need for planning.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.355
Persistent volumes, environment variables, network bindings, ports, credentials,

00:00:33.355 --> 00:00:36.800
reverse proxies, and update processes still need deliberate choices.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.100
A container that starts successfully is not automatically a durable or secure deployment.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.200
Persistent storage should be planned from the beginning.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.500
Decide what needs to survive restarts and upgrades: application configuration,

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.950
user records, conversations, uploaded files, knowledge data,

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:52.400
and model connection settings.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.084
Backups should focus on the data that is hard to recreate.

00:00:55.084 --> 00:00:59.055
If the deployment supports important workflows, test recovery before you need it.

00:00:59.055 --> 00:01:02.495
Installation choices should match privacy, reliability, and maintenance goals.

00:01:02.495 --> 00:01:06.600
A single-user lab can be simpler and more permissive than a multi-user environment.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.890
A private team deployment may need stronger authentication, documented endpoint approval,

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.100
and scheduled updates.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.000
The best setup is not the fanciest one.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.140
It is the one that operators can maintain, protect, and recover.
